+/**
+ * Added by patches/0004-display-site-translation.patch. Several display strings
+ * carry `terminology.singular` ("pull request" / "merge request" / "change
+ * request") or `terminology.shortLabel` ("PR" / "MR" / "change request") as a
+ * runtime-inserted parameter, so the plugin cannot translate them (the value
+ * comes from a function call, not a literal) and the runtime never re-looks-up a
+ * captured value (handoff/T05.md 已知问题 4).
+ *
+ * Step 1 asks the dictionary for the whole string; that covers the fixed labels
+ * and the reviewer-built combinations that have exact `messages` entries. It
+ * deliberately does **not** stand in for argument safety: every text that reaches
+ * step 2 (or falls out of step 1) is still matched against the templates, and a
+ * template can rewrite a parameter. The gate is what decides which whole strings
+ * are looked up at all.
+ *
+ * Step 2 is the reviewed one, for texts whose term arrives as a runtime value.
+ * Step 1 has already run `t(text)` — which is what turns the reviewer-built
+ * combinations (`Commit, push & PR`, …) into whole Chinese sentences via their
+ * exact `messages` entries.
+ *
+ * The remaining strings carry the term inside a sentence the dictionary only knows
+ * as a `{request}` template, so `t(text)` holds the term in English (or, once the
+ * term has already been dealt with, still leaves the sentence English). Step 2
+ * therefore substitutes the translated term into its positions and looks the
+ * result up again so the sentence's own template can build the Chinese text. It
+ * substitutes term-by-term and only outside the parameters listed in `preserve`,
+ * so a term that also occurs inside another parameter does not split it: without
+ * that, providerName "My change request host" reached the dialog as
+ * "解析 My 变更请求 host 变更请求，然后…" and the provider name stopped appearing
+ * at all. `preserve` entries are echoed back verbatim, so they must themselves
+ * contain the whole parameter, not a piece of it (the caller passes the finished
+ * provider name, never a raw value it then decorates).
+ *
+ * The Chinese term does not re-match any `{request}` capture, but the sentence
+ * around it does, which is why the second lookup — not `t(text)` — produces the
+ * Chinese sentence; conversely `t(text.replaceAll(singular, …))` re-ran the
+ * dictionary over a sentence that already held the Chinese term, which is what
+ * risked translating it twice.
+ *
+ * Unlike the progress stream (which the contract lets the server fill with an
+ * arbitrary label, so `GitActionsControl.tsx` reviews an exact list), this helper
+ * is reached only from a reviewed finite set of client literals and built-in
+ * labels: fixed labels ("Branch"), fixed fallback sentences, and template
+ * literals whose dynamic parts are the provider's term, the default branch name
+ * (`Push to <branch>` / `Commit & push to <branch>`,
+ * `resolveDefaultBranchActionDialogCopy`) and `PullRequestThreadDialog`'s
+ * `sourceControlPresentation.providerName`. The branch name survives because the
+ * dictionary's reviewed `Push to {branch}` / `… on "{branch}"` templates echo
+ * their capture, and the provider name survives through `preserve`;
+ * `plugin/__tests__/display-site-translation.test.ts` pins both, so these callers
+ * must not grow new dynamic parts without a test. The two guarantees differ in
+ * strength and the tests say so: a parameter the template captures comes back
+ * verbatim by construction, while `preserve` protects a value only from this
+ * helper's own substitution — a branch named after the term itself
+ * (`my change request branch`) still has that word replaced, which is the
+ * narrower case the reviewed call sites accept. A dynamic part that does *not*
+ * reach its parameter's position — an error message interpolated into a draft
+ * thread title, say — would be dropped from the replaced text, which is why the
+ * caller's option is restricted to the reviewed provider name.
+ *
+ * The `terminology`/`singular`/`shortLabel` values used for comparison, lookup,
+ * or requests stay English in their definition objects. The term used for the
+ * substitution is `t(terminology.singular)` — the dictionary value for the
+ * provider's own term (`pull request` / `merge request` / `change request`),
+ * which is also the `{request}` capture the reviewed sentences carry. A term the
+ * dictionary does not know is left untouched, and with no `__t3zh` (or in en)
+ * both steps return the original text unchanged.
